image/upload/t_thumb/scf4nx.jpg"}, {"id": 705888, "url": "//images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_thumb/scf4o0.jpg"}, {"id": 705893, "url": "//images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_thumb/scf4o5.jpg"}, {"id": 705890, "url": "//images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_thumb/scf4o2.jpg"}, {"id": 705892, "url": "//images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_thumb/scf4o4.jpg"}], "slug": "osu-karate-bu", "storyline": "Tadashi Matsushita has recently entered the Kansai Fifth Technical High School and joined its notorious karate club. There he meets his captain, Yoshiyuki Takagi, an incredibly strong individual. Feared and respected throughout Osaka, Takagi is deep down an honorable and compassionate guy despite his violent endeavors.", "summary": "A Super Famicom fighter game based on the delinquent martial arts manga of the same name. It was developed and published by Culture Brain in 1994.\n\nThe game is a standard martial arts one-on-one fighter in most respects, though features a few uncommon aspects. The first of which is that the player's health bar does not replenish after each round, and the fight continues as each bar is depleted. The second is that the player can recover from losing all their health if they have enough on their power (or qi) gauge. The game counts to three, like in professional wrestling, and the player has the chance to recover a small amount of health in exchange for their power.", "themes": [{"id": 1, "name": "Action"}], "url": "https://www.igdb.com/games/osu-karate-bu", "videos": [{"id": 62947, "name": "Gameplay Video", "video_id": "kceSbTjqzYI", "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kceSbTjqzYI", "title": "SNES Longplay [425] Ossu!! Karate bu", "thumb": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/kceSbTjqzYI/hqdefault.jpg"}]}